######## FINDING 1 — ARPs RECEIVED are answered but NOT cached ########
Injected 28 ARP requests (op=1, who-has 10.38.215.66, from 28 distinct sender
IP+MAC pairs) into MEMBER 1's port (port1.0.1, vlan3001) via tb470 eth3.

  frames arrived     : YES - port1.0.1 input counters advanced
  stack replied      : YES - to EVERY request, ~500-600us, e.g.
       02:00:00:aa:00:43 > ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff  Request who-has 10.38.215.66 tell 10.38.215.67
       00:00:cd:37:0d:6f > 02:00:00:aa:00:43  Reply 10.38.215.66 is-at 00:00:cd:37:0d:6f
  ARP entries created: **NONE** - `show arp` remained empty
  reply source MAC   : 00:00:cd:37:0d:6f = the stack VMAC, NOT either member's real
                       MAC, even though the ingress port is on the BACKUP member.

=> The stack answers ARP requests for its own address but does NOT learn the sender.
   Verified positively (capture shows the replies) - this is not absence of evidence.
   Entries appear ONLY when the stack itself resolves an address (e.g. it must reply
   to an ICMP echo, so it resolves the pinging host).

######## FINDING 2 — entries the stack DOES create ARE fully synced ########
Created entries the way the stack actually creates them: pinged the stack from tb470
eth3 (-> member 1's port) and eth2 (-> member 2's port).

  show arp (stack-wide control plane):
    10.38.215.33  00f0.4d00.7717  vlan3002  port2.0.1  dynamic   [learned on MEMBER 2]
    10.38.215.65  00f0.4d00.7718  vlan3001  port1.0.1  dynamic   [learned on MEMBER 1]

  show platform table ip  (PER-MEMBER ASIC tables - the real test of syncing):
    Stack member 1 [Instance 0]:
      10.38.215.33  32  00f0.4d00.7717  Dev 1  Port 1  VID 3002  Ena
      10.38.215.65  32  00f0.4d00.7718  Dev 0  Port 1  VID 3001  Ena
    Stack member 2 [Instance 1]:
      10.38.215.33  32  00f0.4d00.7717  Dev 1  Port 1  VID 3002  Ena
      10.38.215.65  32  00f0.4d00.7718  Dev 0  Port 1  VID 3001  Ena

=> IDENTICAL on both members, including the `Dev` field naming the OTHER member as the
   egress device. ARP/host entries ARE synchronised stack-wide into hardware, and it
   makes NO difference whether the entry was learned on the master's port or the
   backup's port.

=> ANSWER TO THE OBJECTIVE: the ARP entry IS synced across the stack - but only for
   entries the stack creates itself. A received ARP request creates nothing to sync.

